1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,993
In the previous video you answered this by
reading commits, the saved snapshots of

2
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:13,193
the project. You guessed that the fault
was in normalization, you blamed the

3
00:00:13,233 --> 00:00:17,726
function, you looked through the formatter.
That worked because you had an idea of

4
00:00:17,766 --> 00:00:18,500
where to look.

5
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:23,615
Now remove the idea. The fault is in a file
nobody suspects, or the diff between the

6
00:00:23,655 --> 00:00:27,571
last good release and today is four
thousand commits by sixty people.

7
00:00:27,611 --> 00:00:32,329
Reading doesn't scale. What you do have is
a test: one command that says yes or no.

8
00:00:32,369 --> 00:00:36,915
With a test and two endpoints, Git can
find the commit in about twelve steps for

9
00:00:36,955 --> 00:00:40,642
four thousand commits, without you
understanding the code at all.

10
00:00:42,482 --> 00:00:46,373
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:46,413 --> 00:00:50,820
This is step 6 of the method from
the last video: search by behavior.

12
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:56,169
The tool is git bisect. Today you run one
bisection by hand, verdict by verdict, so

13
00:00:56,209 --> 00:01:01,066
that you see every piece of state it
creates. In the next video you hand the

14
00:01:01,106 --> 00:01:02,420
verdicts to a script.

15
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:08,063
You need two things from earlier in the
course. From the detached HEAD video: what

16
00:01:08,103 --> 00:01:13,211
it means that HEAD, Git's name for where
you are, names a commit and no branch.

17
00:01:13,251 --> 00:01:17,967
From the git log video: the range
v0.1.0..main, which had twenty commits.

18
00:01:18,007 --> 00:01:21,812
You were told to keep that
number. Here's where it's spent.

19
00:01:23,652 --> 00:01:28,788
explain why a bisection over n commits
needs about log2(n) tests, and what it

20
00:01:28,828 --> 00:01:29,325
assumes;

21
00:01:29,665 --> 00:01:32,563
run a manual bisection with
a good and a bad commit;

22
00:01:32,903 --> 00:01:36,565
skip a commit that cannot be tested,
and read what Git reports then;

23
00:01:36,905 --> 00:01:41,686
read the bisect state from its refs and
its log, and end the session cleanly.

24
00:01:43,526 --> 00:01:47,853
In one sentence: 🟡 CAUTION, git bisect
finds the commit that changed a property by

25
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:52,219
checking out a commit in the middle of the
suspects, asking you for a verdict, and

26
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:54,296
halving the suspects until one is left.

27
00:01:54,636 --> 00:01:59,806
The label is CAUTION and not SAFE because
bisect detaches HEAD and checks out other

28
00:01:59,846 --> 00:02:04,263
commits into your working tree, and
it writes refs and files into .git.

29
00:02:04,303 --> 00:02:09,159
A ref is a name that holds a commit ID.
It's recoverable with one command, git

30
00:02:09,199 --> 00:02:09,975
bisect reset.

31
00:02:10,315 --> 00:02:14,930
Precisely. You give one commit that has the
property, called "bad" or "new", and at

32
00:02:14,970 --> 00:02:19,528
least one ancestor, an earlier commit in
its history, that lacks it, called "good"

33
00:02:19,568 --> 00:02:24,239
or "old". The candidates are the commits
reachable from the bad one and not from any

34
00:02:24,279 --> 00:02:24,744
good one.

35
00:02:25,084 --> 00:02:28,189
That's a range, the same
two-dot range you already know.

36
00:02:28,229 --> 00:02:32,625
Git assumes there is a single first bad
commit: in the manual's words, "all its

37
00:02:32,665 --> 00:02:35,994
descendants are 'bad' and all
the other commits are 'good'".

38
00:02:36,034 --> 00:02:40,318
At each step it picks the candidate that
splits the remaining candidates most

39
00:02:40,358 --> 00:02:40,711
evenly.

40
00:02:41,051 --> 00:02:44,200
The arithmetic. Every verdict
halves the candidates.

41
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:49,044
So k verdicts can separate two to the
power k candidates, and N candidates need

42
00:02:49,084 --> 00:02:50,577
about log2 of N verdicts.

43
00:02:50,917 --> 00:02:56,282
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: start
from 4,000 and keep halving until one is

44
00:02:56,322 --> 00:02:59,306
left. Count the halvings,
then say your answer.

45
00:03:04,146 --> 00:03:06,841
About twelve. That's the
number from the opening.

46
00:03:07,181 --> 00:03:11,619
Doubling the history adds one test. This
is why "it worked in the last release" is

47
00:03:11,659 --> 00:03:14,567
enough of a starting point,
however long ago that was.

48
00:03:14,907 --> 00:03:18,920
Inside .git, a bisection is
state, kept until you end it.

49
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:24,893
Every verdict is a ref under refs/bisect/.
A file BISECT_START holds where to return

50
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:30,581
to, BISECT_TERMS holds the two words in
use, and BISECT_LOG holds the session so

51
00:03:30,621 --> 00:03:30,865
far.

52
00:03:31,205 --> 00:03:34,595
When to use it: when reading
gives no suspect, or several.

53
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:39,208
When not to: don't bisect what you can
read. If the pickaxe or blame names one

54
00:03:39,248 --> 00:03:43,585
commit, and a test at that commit and
its parent confirms it, you're done.

55
00:03:43,625 --> 00:03:48,552
And don't bisect before you have checked
that the known-good commit is still good in

56
00:03:48,592 --> 00:03:53,461
today's environment. If the cause is a
dependency that floated to a new version, or

57
00:03:53,501 --> 00:03:58,428
a data file outside Git, every commit may
be bad and bisect will report whatever the

58
00:03:58,468 --> 00:03:59,256
noise selects.

59
00:03:59,596 --> 00:04:04,131
One more requirement. Bisect needs
commits that can be tested one by one.

60
00:04:04,171 --> 00:04:09,395
A history of small commits that each build
gives an answer you can read in a minute.

61
00:04:09,435 --> 00:04:14,220
A history of large squashes gives an
answer that is a two-thousand-line diff.

62
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:20,631
Now a picture to keep. The textbook's
analogy is looking up a word in a printed

63
00:04:20,671 --> 00:04:25,474
dictionary. Open in the middle, see whether
your word comes before or after, repeat

64
00:04:25,514 --> 00:04:26,350
with that half.

65
00:04:26,690 --> 00:04:30,899
The analogy breaks in two places, and
both are where bisections go wrong.

66
00:04:31,239 --> 00:04:35,397
First, history is a graph, not a list.
"The middle" has to be computed.

67
00:04:35,437 --> 00:04:40,127
With a merge in the range, a commit that
joins two lines of work, the commit Git

68
00:04:40,167 --> 00:04:43,911
picks isn't the one you would pick
by counting lines of git log.

69
00:04:44,251 --> 00:04:48,720
Second, a dictionary is sorted by
construction. "Good before, bad after" is an

70
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:52,074
assumption about your project,
and bisect can't verify it.

71
00:04:52,114 --> 00:04:56,872
If the bug was introduced, fixed and
introduced again, there is more than one first

72
00:04:56,912 --> 00:05:01,266
bad commit. Bisect finds one transition
and can't tell you there are others.

73
00:05:03,606 --> 00:05:07,338
So every verdict you give is a
statement of fact about one property.

74
00:05:07,378 --> 00:05:11,275
"The runner crashed" is not the same
statement as "the score is wrong".

75
00:05:11,315 --> 00:05:13,715
Keep that distinction
for the demonstration.

76
00:05:15,555 --> 00:05:19,212
In the demonstration, watch
the order in which Git asks.

77
00:05:19,252 --> 00:05:24,560
Step 1 lands near the middle and gets no
verdict. Step 2 jumps to the right half.

78
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:30,105
Step 3 jumps far left. After that, each step
lands inside a range that is about half

79
00:05:30,145 --> 00:05:31,822
as long as the one before.

80
00:05:36,162 --> 00:05:41,139
Predict: twenty candidates. Roughly
how many steps will Git announce?

81
00:05:41,179 --> 00:05:43,102
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

82
00:05:47,942 --> 00:05:51,384
"9 revisions left to test
after this (roughly 3 steps)".

83
00:05:51,424 --> 00:05:56,608
Git checked out 074d492. That commit and
its ancestors account for ten of the twenty

84
00:05:56,648 --> 00:06:01,396
candidates, so either verdict leaves ten
suspects, one of which already has a

85
00:06:01,436 --> 00:06:06,247
verdict. The step count is an estimate
computed from the number of candidates.

86
00:06:06,587 --> 00:06:10,221
Here are the candidates on
the first-parent line again.

87
00:06:10,261 --> 00:06:13,227
HEAD now points at
074d492, and at no branch.

88
00:06:13,567 --> 00:06:19,464
HEAD is detached at 074d492, and git status
tells you that you're bisecting and how

89
00:06:19,504 --> 00:06:21,610
to get back. Now run the test.

90
00:06:23,950 --> 00:06:28,052
The output will be a KeyError. Before you
see the next command, decide: is this

91
00:06:28,092 --> 00:06:29,048
commit good or bad?

92
00:06:31,388 --> 00:06:36,010
Neither. The runner crashes for a reason
that has nothing to do with scoring.

93
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:40,248
Calling this "bad" would be a false
statement about the property under

94
00:06:40,288 --> 00:06:45,030
investigation. git bisect skip says "no
verdict", and Git picks another commit,

95
00:06:45,070 --> 00:06:47,996
d9d075d. If you said bad,
you're in good company.

96
00:06:48,337 --> 00:06:53,355
On the picture: a skip, and HEAD on
d9d075d. From here the test answers.

97
00:06:53,695 --> 00:07:00,799
exact_match=0.400: bad. Git moves
to 72134f3, far to the left.

98
00:07:01,139 --> 00:07:06,582
exact_match=0.800: good. Three
revisions left after this one.

99
00:07:06,622 --> 00:07:08,200
Two more verdicts.

100
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:13,398
On the picture so far: a skip, a bad,
a good, and HEAD now on 9c8df98.

101
00:07:13,738 --> 00:07:17,857
The formatter commit 9c8df98
is bad. be9ad1b is good.

102
00:07:17,897 --> 00:07:24,057
Git now says "0 revisions left to test
after this" and has checked out d926d3c.

103
00:07:24,397 --> 00:07:30,828
On the picture, two more marks: 9c8df98
is bad, be9ad1b is good, and HEAD sits on

104
00:07:30,868 --> 00:07:31,467
d926d3c.

105
00:07:33,807 --> 00:07:37,930
Predict: how many more verdicts
are needed? Look at the diagram.

106
00:07:37,970 --> 00:07:43,199
A good commit on the left of d926d3c, a
bad commit on its right, and nothing else

107
00:07:43,239 --> 00:07:44,045
between them.

108
00:07:44,385 --> 00:07:48,386
One. "d926d3c5a77b586297eb25335c33fed4aee6f053
is the first 'bad' commit", and Git

109
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:49,272
prints the commit.

110
00:07:49,612 --> 00:07:54,171
Twenty candidates, five verdicts and one
skip. That's the number twenty, spent.

111
00:07:54,211 --> 00:07:57,547
And it's the same commit that
blame and the pickaxe named.

112
00:07:57,587 --> 00:08:00,748
Bisect got there without
knowing which file to look at.

113
00:08:01,088 --> 00:08:03,602
Don't reset yet. Look at the state.

114
00:08:03,942 --> 00:08:09,447
One ref named refs/bisect/bad, one ref per
good commit with the full ID in its name,

115
00:08:09,487 --> 00:08:13,276
and one per skipped commit.
And the BISECT_ files in .git.

116
00:08:13,316 --> 00:08:16,247
Those refs are the labels
you watched appear.

117
00:08:16,587 --> 00:08:20,993
git bisect log prints the session as
commands that would reproduce it, with

118
00:08:21,033 --> 00:08:24,729
comments. This is evidence, so
paste it into the incident note.

119
00:08:24,769 --> 00:08:26,626
In the next video you replay it.

120
00:08:29,966 --> 00:08:32,633
Back on main, no refs under refs/bisect.

121
00:08:38,973 --> 00:08:42,999
Read the fifth column of every row:
the current branch ref never moves.

122
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:46,607
A bisection is an inspection.
Your branch is where you left it.

123
00:08:48,447 --> 00:08:50,095
Five mistakes to watch for.

124
00:08:50,435 --> 00:08:55,132
Marking a commit that crashes as "bad".
Root cause: a verdict is a statement about

125
00:08:55,172 --> 00:08:59,811
one property, and a crash for another
reason is "no verdict"; the false statement

126
00:08:59,851 --> 00:09:01,949
sends the search into the wrong half.

127
00:09:02,289 --> 00:09:07,280
Starting a bisection with uncommitted
changes. Root cause: Git refuses to check out

128
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:11,402
the next commit over uncommitted
changes to files it has to replace.

129
00:09:11,742 --> 00:09:16,511
Trusting the result when the bug came and
went. Root cause: bisect assumes a single

130
00:09:16,551 --> 00:09:21,204
transition from good to bad and cannot
detect that the property is not monotonic;

131
00:09:21,244 --> 00:09:24,044
test the reported commit
and its parent yourself.

132
00:09:24,384 --> 00:09:27,839
Skipping commits next to the
culprit and expecting one answer.

133
00:09:27,879 --> 00:09:32,517
Root cause: the manual states that if you
skip a commit adjacent to the one you are

134
00:09:32,557 --> 00:09:37,253
looking for, Git cannot tell which of them
was the first bad one, and it ends with a

135
00:09:37,293 --> 00:09:38,324
list of candidates.

136
00:09:38,664 --> 00:09:43,636
Forgetting git bisect reset. Root cause:
until the reset, HEAD is detached, and

137
00:09:43,676 --> 00:09:46,618
commits made in that state
belong to no branch.

138
00:09:48,458 --> 00:09:53,562
Now, out of the lab. A team that serves a
retrieval model sees latency at the 95th

139
00:09:53,602 --> 00:09:57,011
percentile rise by a third
between two weekly releases.

140
00:09:57,051 --> 00:10:02,155
About four hundred commits lie between the
two release tags, from the service, the

141
00:10:02,195 --> 00:10:06,044
tokenizer wrapper and the batching
code. Nobody has a suspect.

142
00:10:06,384 --> 00:10:11,287
One engineer reduces the symptom to a
single command: replay two hundred recorded

143
00:10:11,327 --> 00:10:15,619
requests against a local build and
compare one number with a threshold.

144
00:10:15,659 --> 00:10:20,379
She first runs it on last week's tag, on
today's machine, to confirm that last

145
00:10:20,419 --> 00:10:24,468
week's tag is still good. It is,
so the cause is in the repository.

146
00:10:24,808 --> 00:10:28,802
Then she bisects. Four hundred
candidates need about nine verdicts.

147
00:10:28,842 --> 00:10:33,620
Two commits don't build because a generated
file is missing, and she skips them.

148
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:37,052
The result is a commit that
changed a default batch size.

149
00:10:37,092 --> 00:10:41,990
She tests that commit and its parent once
more by hand, attaches the bisect log to

150
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:45,001
the incident note, and only
then opens the revert.

151
00:10:46,841 --> 00:10:54,537
Your turn. Do Lab 11.4, "A manual bisect",
in lab-manual/m11-history-forensics.md.

152
00:10:54,877 --> 00:10:59,373
Before you start, count the candidates and
predict how many verdicts you'll need.

153
00:10:59,413 --> 00:11:04,022
At every step, before you run the test,
predict whether Git has moved left or right

154
00:11:04,062 --> 00:11:08,446
of the previous commit and why. The lab's
failure scenario has you give a wrong

155
00:11:08,486 --> 00:11:12,254
verdict. Predict where the search
will end before you let it finish.

156
00:11:12,594 --> 00:11:19,274
The challenge is Exercise 11.9, Level 4,
"The regression among commits that cannot

157
00:11:19,314 --> 00:11:24,109
be tested", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

158
00:11:25,949 --> 00:11:27,226
Pause and answer out loud.

159
00:11:32,066 --> 00:11:36,266
A strong answer gives the number with
its reasoning, not from memory: what one

160
00:11:36,306 --> 00:11:40,832
verdict does to the candidate set. It states
the assumption in the manual's terms, a

161
00:11:40,872 --> 00:11:45,181
single first bad commit, and names at
least two ways a real project violates it.

162
00:11:45,521 --> 00:11:50,176
It then describes a check that needs no
new tool: what you test by hand, at which

163
00:11:50,216 --> 00:11:53,769
two commits, and what you read
to audit the verdicts you gave.

164
00:11:53,809 --> 00:11:58,291
The best answers also ask a question back
before they start: is the known-good

165
00:11:58,331 --> 00:12:01,073
commit still good today,
in today's environment?

166
00:12:02,913 --> 00:12:05,840
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

167
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:11,044
A bisection halves the candidates with
every verdict, so N commits need about

168
00:12:11,084 --> 00:12:11,935
log2(N) tests.

169
00:12:12,275 --> 00:12:16,283
It assumes one transition from good
to bad; it cannot verify that.

170
00:12:16,623 --> 00:12:19,936
A commit that cannot be tested
is skipped, never called bad.

171
00:12:20,276 --> 00:12:24,561
The session is state: refs under refs/bisect/
and BISECT_ files, printed by git

172
00:12:24,601 --> 00:12:26,915
bisect log and removed
by git bisect reset.

173
00:12:27,255 --> 00:12:30,637
The branch I started from does
not move during a bisection.

174
00:12:32,477 --> 00:12:35,866
Read section 14A.20 of Chapter 14A.

175
00:12:36,206 --> 00:12:40,427
Today you found one commit among
twenty with five verdicts, by hand.

176
00:12:40,467 --> 00:12:45,566
Do the manual bisect lab before the next
video. Next time: git bisect run, where a

177
00:12:45,606 --> 00:12:50,517
script gives the verdicts. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

178
00:12:50,557 --> 00:12:52,021
See you in the next one.
